<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/16th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Chaldiran</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1514">1514</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Chaldiran · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-persian-wars">Ottoman–Persian Wars</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Chaldiran was fought in 1514 in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> between the Ottoman sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selim-i">Selim I</a> and the Safavid shah <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismail-i">Ismail I</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, using artillery and firearms, won a decisive victory over the Safavid cavalry. The defeat halted Safavid expansion into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> and fixed a long-term frontier between the two empires.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-chaldiran.jpg" alt="Battle of Chaldiran" width="320" height="342" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Amir Pashaei · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_painting_in_Chehel_Sotoun1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After his struggle against his brothers for the Ottoman throne, Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/selim-i">Selim I</a> turned to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qizilbash">Qizilbash</a>, a Twelver Shi'i warrior-dervish group that had sided with other members of the dynasty against him. He feared they would incite the population against him in favour of their leader, the Safavid ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismail-i">Ismail I</a>, whom his partisans believed to be descended from Muhammad and infallible. Selim obtained a jurist's opinion describing Ismail and the Qizilbash as unbelievers and heretics, and before the campaign he ordered the execution of some 40,000 Qizilbash in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>. He also tried to block the import of Iranian silk.</p><p>When Selim marched east, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khanate-of-bukhara">Khanate of Bukhara</a> attacked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavids</a> from the other side, and Ismail used a scorched earth policy against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>. Selim's army was discontented, because supply was hard, the terrain of the Armenian highlands was rough and the men were marching against fellow Muslims. At one point the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janissaries">janissaries</a> fired their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">muskets</a> at the sultan's tent. Learning that the Safavid army was forming at Chaldiran, Selim moved quickly to fight, partly to quiet this discontent.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The battle took place on 23 August 1514. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> had a larger army, of 60,000 to 100,000 men, and many heavy artillery pieces. The Safavid army numbered 40,000 to 80,000 and had no artillery. The Ottomans placed their guns and thousands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janissaries">janissaries</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a> weapons behind a barrier of carts. The Safavid cavalry attacked the wings to avoid the guns at the centre, but the artillery was highly mobile and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavids</a> suffered disastrous losses. Ismail was wounded and almost captured, and his wives were taken by Selim. Kurdish tribes fought on both sides, since the battle was fought in the Kurdish heartland.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> entered the Safavid capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a> on 7 September, pillaged it and left, though the Friday sermon that week was delivered in Selim's name. Selim could not press further because of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janissaries">janissaries</a>' discontent. The Ottomans annexed Eastern Anatolia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-mesopotamia">Upper Mesopotamia</a>. Control of these regions changed hands several times, was settled in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-amasya">Peace of Amasya</a> of 1555 and was made firm by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-zuhab">Treaty of Zuhab</a> in 1639. Ismail withdrew from government and never again took part in a military campaign.</p><p>The defeat undermined belief in the infallibility of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qizilbash">Qizilbash</a> leader, and Kurdish chiefs shifted their allegiance from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavids</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>. The Safavids made firearms a part of their armies, and Ismail's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tahmasp-i">Tahmasp I</a> used <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a> in later battles. Selim next turned south against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate</a> in 1516 and 1517.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Chaldiran” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Aq Qoyunlu partisans held Baghdad until 1508 and Murad died in exile some years later, while Diyarbakır passed to the Safavids and then, after the Ottoman victory at the <b>Battle of Chaldiran</b> in 1514, to the Ottoman Empire.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Safavid military changed fundamentally between the empire's founding and the reign of Shah Abbas I, and the cause was the hard lesson of the <b>Battle of Chaldiran</b> in 1514.</blockquote><a href="/empire/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>At <b>Chaldiran</b> in 1514, Ottoman guns and Janissary musketeers defeated the cavalry of the Safavid shah Ismail I. In 1516 and 1517, at Marj Dabiq and Ridaniya, the same weapons broke the Mamluk Sultanate and brought Syria and Egypt under Ottoman rule.</blockquote><a href="/essays/the-military-revolution">The Military Revolution</a></li></ul></section>
